Task 4.4 – Prevent Recurrence (1 Mark)
The @Override annotation tells the Java compiler that a method is intended to override a method from its superclass or interface.

In the original program, the developer writes:
@Override
public void mouseclicked(MouseEvent e)


Since mouseclicked() does not match the correctly named mouseClicked() method in MouseAdapter, the compiler reports an error.

Expected compiler message:
error: method does not override or implement
a method from a supertype

@Override
^


Without @Override, the misspelled method may compile as an ordinary method, but it will not receive mouse-click events.

Conclusion: Using @Override helps identify spelling mistakes and incorrect method signatures during compilation instead of allowing them to become runtime bugs.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
1. Alternative Approach: Using BufferedImage
Instead of storing individual points in an ArrayList<Point>, the program can use an off-screen BufferedImage to store the complete drawing.

A BufferedImage acts as an invisible drawing surface. Whenever the user clicks, the application draws a dot directly onto this image.

During repainting, the image is copied onto the window.
Implementation

import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.*;
import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;

public class DrawPadImage extends Frame {

    private BufferedImage image;
    private int count = 0;

    private Label status = new Label("Clicks: 0");

    public DrawPadImage() {

        super("Draw Pad - BufferedImage");

        setLayout(new BorderLayout());
        add(status, BorderLayout.SOUTH);

        image = new BufferedImage(
            400, 300,
            BufferedImage.TYPE_INT_ARGB
        );

        addMouseListener(new MouseAdapter() {

            @Override
            public void mouseClicked(MouseEvent e) {

                Graphics g = image.getGraphics();

                g.fillOval(
                    e.getX() - 3,
                    e.getY() - 3,
                    6,
                    6
                );

                g.dispose();

                count++;
                status.setText("Clicks: " + count);

                repaint();
            }
        });

        addWindowListener(new WindowAdapter() {
            @Override
            public void windowClosing(WindowEvent e) {
                dispose();
            }
        });

        setSize(400, 300);
    }

    @Override
    public void paint(Graphics g) {

        super.paint(g);

        g.drawImage(image, 0, 0, this);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new DrawPadImage().setVisible(true);
    }
}



Working:
1. The application creates an off-screen BufferedImage.
2. When the user clicks, a dot is drawn onto the image.
3. The click count is updated.
4. repaint() requests a redraw.
5. The paint() method copies the stored image onto the window.
6. The dots remain visible after minimizing and restoring the window.
Implementation note: This example uses a fixed-size drawing image. A production version should resize or recreate the image while preserving its previous contents when the window size changes.
2. Time Complexity Analysis
Let:
- n = Number of clicks.
- W = Width of the drawing area.
- H = Height of the drawing area.
Operation	ArrayList approach	BufferedImage approach
Store a new click	O(1) amortized	O(1) pixel drawing
Repaint the window	O(n)	O(W × H) for image copying
Redraw previous dots	O(n)	Already stored in the image
Update click count	O(1)	O(1)

ArrayList approach:

Every repaint requires looping through all n stored points.
\[
T(n)=O(n)
\]

BufferedImage approach:

The application copies the complete image onto the window.
\[
T(W,H)=O(W\times H)
\]

The image approach avoids redrawing each individual dot during repainting, but the image-copying operation still depends on the number of pixels.
3. Space Complexity Analysis
Approach	Space complexity	Explanation
ArrayList of Points	O(n)	Stores the coordinates of every click.
BufferedImage	O(W × H)	Stores pixel information for the complete drawing area.

ArrayList:

Each click requires storing one Point object containing x and y coordinates.
\[
S(n)=O(n)
\]

BufferedImage:

The image stores pixel information for the complete drawing area.
\[
S(W,H)=O(W\times H)
\]

For a 400 × 300 image using 32-bit pixels, the raw pixel storage is approximately:
\[
400\times300\times4=480000\text{ bytes}
\]

Approximately 468.75 KiB, excluding object and image-management overhead.
4. Comparison of Both Approaches
Parameter	ArrayList of Points	BufferedImage
Drawing storage	Individual coordinates	Complete pixel image
Repainting	Redraws all points	Copies stored image
Repaint time	O(n)	O(W × H)
Space complexity	O(n)	O(W × H)
Adding a dot	Stores coordinates	Draws directly onto image
Persistent drawing	Yes	Yes
Editing individual dots	Easier	More difficult
Suitable for	Simple drawing applications	Frequent repainting and image-based drawing

Final Conclusion
The ArrayList approach is suitable for a simple drawing pad because it stores only the clicked coordinates and makes individual points easy to manage. The BufferedImage approach is more suitable when the drawing contains many marks and frequent repainting is expected. It avoids looping through every stored point during repainting, but requires memory proportional to the image dimensions.
